      "text": "To determine the radius of the circle, we start by noting that the center of the circle lies on the x-axis, which means the y-coordinate of the center is 0. Let the center of the circle be \\((h, 0)\\). The distance from the center \\((h, 0)\\) to the point \\((0, 4)\\) is equal to the distance from the center \\((h, 0)\\) to the point \\((1, 3)\\). This distance can be expressed using the distance formula.\n\nThe distance from \\((h, 0)\\) to \\((0, 4)\\) is:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(h - 0)^2 + (0 - 4)^2} = \\sqrt{h^2 + 16}\n\\]\n\nThe distance from \\((h, 0)\\) to \\((1, 3)\\) is:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(h - 1)^2 + (0 - 3)^2} = \\sqrt{(h - 1)^2 + 9}\n\\]\n\nSince these distances are equal, we set up the equation:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{h^2 + 16} = \\sqrt{(h - 1)^2 + 9}\n\\]\n\nTo eliminate the square roots, we square both sides of the equation:\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = (h - 1)^2 + 9\n\\]\n\nNext, we expand the right side of the equation:\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = h^2 - 2h + 1 + 9\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the right side, we get:\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = h^2 - 2h + 10\n\\]\n\nWe can subtract \\(h^2\\) from both sides:\n\\[\n16 = -2h + 10\n\\]\n\nSubtract 10 from both sides:\n\\[\n6 = -2h\n\\]\n\nDivide both sides by -2:\n\\[\nh = -3\n\\]\n\nSo, the center of the circle is \\((-3, 0)\\). Now, we need to find the radius of the circle, which is the distance from the center \\((-3, 0)\\) to either of the points \\((0, 4)\\) or \\((1, 3)\\). We use the distance formula to find the distance from \\((-3, 0)\\) to \\((0, 4)\\):\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(-3 - 0)^2 + (0 - 4)^2} = \\sqrt{(-3)^2 + (-4)^2} = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = \\sqrt{25} = 5\n\\]\n\nThus, the radius of the circle is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
