      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine the radius of the circle, we start by noting that the center of the circle lies on the x-axis. Let the center of the circle be \\((h, 0)\\). Since the circle passes through the points \\((0, 4)\\) and \\((1, 3)\\), we can use the distance formula to set up equations based on the distance from the center to each of these points.\n\nFirst, the distance from the center \\((h, 0)\\) to \\((0, 4)\\) is the radius \\(r\\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[\nr = \\sqrt{(h - 0)^2 + (0 - 4)^2} = \\sqrt{h^2 + 16}\n\\]\n\nNext, the distance from the center \\((h, 0)\\) to \\((1, 3)\\) is also the radius \\(r\\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[\nr = \\sqrt{(h - 1)^2 + (0 - 3)^2} = \\sqrt{(h - 1)^2 + 9}\n\\]\n\nSince both expressions are equal to the radius \\(r\\), we can set them equal to each other:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{h^2 + 16} = \\sqrt{(h - 1)^2 + 9}\n\\]\n\nTo eliminate the square roots, we square both sides of the equation:\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = (h - 1)^2 + 9\n\\]\n\nExpanding the right-hand side, we get:\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = h^2 - 2h + 1 + 9\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the right-hand side, we have:\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = h^2 - 2h + 10\n\\]\n\nSubtracting \\(h^2\\) from both sides, we get:\n\\[\n16 = -2h + 10\n\\]\n\nSubtracting 10 from both sides, we get:\n\\[\n6 = -2h\n\\]\n\nDividing both sides by -2, we get:\n\\[\nh = -3\n\\]\n\nNow that we have the x-coordinate of the center, we can find the radius \\(r\\) by substituting \\(h = -3\\) back into the expression for the radius:\n\\[\nr = \\sqrt{(-3)^2 + 16} = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = \\sqrt{25} = 5\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circle is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
